  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several basic interaction channels for magnons, showing how spin-wave excitations can scatter, split, or couple to lattice vibrations while conserving energy and momentum. Each cartoon uses colored wavy arrows for incoming and outgoing quasiparticles, labeled by frequency and wave vector.\n\nAt left, two-magnon scattering is depicted as a magnon hitting an inhomogeneity and emerging as another magnon with the same frequency but a different wave vector, indicating elastic scattering caused by disorder or defects. The middle panels show intrinsic nonlinear magnon processes. In three-magnon splitting, one magnon decays into two lower-energy magnons, with the equations below emphasizing that both energy and momentum are shared between the products. In four-magnon scattering, two incoming magnons interact and convert into two different outgoing magnons, again with conservation laws written explicitly.\n\nThe rightmost panel adds the lattice: a magnon scatters into another magnon while emitting or exchanging a phonon, drawn as a small chain of atoms connected by bonds plus a wavy phonon-like branch. Overall, the figure conveys the main ways magnons can redistribute energy and momentum through defects, nonlinear magnon-magnon interactions, and magnon-phonon coupling.",
